{:edb/db-before-t 413 :edb/db-after-t 414 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533726 50 #inst "2026-10-04T19:24:20.077Z" 13194139533726 true] [17592186054648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248673 13194139533726 true] [17592186068604 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248656 13194139533726 true] [17592186087692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248662 13194139533726 true] [17592186087769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248668 13194139533726 true] [17592186129077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248659 13194139533726 true] [17592186133748 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248665 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248657 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1089 "q-167521" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1113 #uuid "d4b74f6b-3fc2-5a2a-85a4-751a2bc73d04" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248656 1145 "Let's consider the right triangle formed by two sides and a diagonal.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/1c97f7121591003f503cfbce056e0709.png)\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2{)}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(x+2{)}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{(x+2{)}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+4-{x}^{2}} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4(x+1)} \\\\ a & =\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ a & =2\\sqrt{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248657 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248658 13194139533726 true] [17592186248657 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248657 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248657 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248658 13194139533726 true] [17592186248657 1110 #uuid "c96791a4-2a69-5048-a5ad-f96a3c2e7576" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248658 1034 "2\\sqrt{x+1}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248658 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248658 1111 #uuid "f894e36e-6da4-5d69-9da2-a7bf627897b9" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248660 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1089 "q-167522" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n$2\\sqrt{x+1}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/q-167522.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the pool is $120$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n." 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1113 #uuid "5592a441-1b83-5b99-8e2a-b81e0a01d953" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248659 1145 "Let's analyze the given polynomial:\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd, and the leading coefficient (${a}_{3}=1$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n* $p(x)→-\\infty $ as $x→-\\infty .$\n\n* $p(x)→\\infty $ as $x→\\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $D$ satisfies these conditions." 13194139533726 true] [17592186248660 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248661 13194139533726 true] [17592186248660 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248660 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248660 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248661 13194139533726 true] [17592186248660 1110 #uuid "ad0fc7b5-593b-5127-9911-2073216bfab5" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248661 1034 "D" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248661 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248661 1111 #uuid "3753eb3a-091b-5d2b-bc2d-961a1b3c4734" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248663 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1089 "q-167523" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n$2\\sqrt{x+1}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/q-167522.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the pool is $120$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n\n$D$\n\n.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nDetermine which of $x=5$, $x=10,$ and $x=15$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\nis a factor of $p(x)$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1113 #uuid "4ee6550e-f908-5e8a-84ca-600f6da3cc3e" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248662 1145 "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$\n\nHere, we consider the numbers $5$, $10,$ and $15$ as possible roots. Let's check each of these values in turn.\n\n* $x=5$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =(5{)}^{3}+(5{)}^{2}-3\\,600 \\\\ & =125+25-3\\,600 \\\\ & =-3\\,450 \\\\ & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=10$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10) & =(10{)}^{3}+(10{)}^{2}-3\\,600 \\\\ & =1\\,000+100-3\\,600 \\\\ & =-2\\,500 \\\\ & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n* $x=15$ is a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(15) & =(15{)}^{3}+(15{)}^{2}-3\\,600 \\\\ & =3\\,375+225-3\\,600 \\\\ & =3\\,600-3\\,600 \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, $(x-15)$ is a factor of $p(x).$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248663 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248664 13194139533726 true] [17592186248663 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248663 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248663 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248664 13194139533726 true] [17592186248663 1110 #uuid "bffaf69a-1dbd-58d7-9e5d-30d234c3854c" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248664 1034 "x-15" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248664 1111 #uuid "971c9794-8c29-5e3a-8967-a65711d0f561" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248666 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1089 "q-167524" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n$2\\sqrt{x+1}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/q-167522.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the pool is $120$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n\n$D$\n\n.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nDetermine which of $x=5$, $x=10,$ and $x=15$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n$x-15$\n\nis a factor of $p(x)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe polynomial $p(x)$ can be factored as\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-15)\\cdot q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial. Find $q(x).$\n\n$q(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1113 #uuid "cd2b3e7f-58a5-5304-9b20-abffb592b225" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248665 1145 "To factor $p(x),$ we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-15)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-15)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-15)$ using synthetic division:\n\n| | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-3600$ |\n| | $↓$ | $15$ | $240$ | $3600$ |\n| | $1$ | $16$ | $240$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600}{x-15}=({x}^{2}+16x+240),\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600=(x-15)({x}^{2}+16x+240).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+16x+240$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac={16}^{2}-4(1)(240)=-704<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial ${x}^{2}+16x+240$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $p(x)$ over the real numbers is given by\n\n$$\n(x-15)({x}^{2}+16x+240).\n$$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248666 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248667 13194139533726 true] [17592186248666 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248666 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248666 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248667 13194139533726 true] [17592186248666 1110 #uuid "5c6840d8-f9c5-5ac3-b919-72bc5c7ecea8" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248667 1034 "x^2+16x+240" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248667 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248667 1111 #uuid "180d2fdb-66f6-5c71-9388-35d3c04da39b" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248669 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248671 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1089 "q-167525" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n$2\\sqrt{x+1}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/q-167522.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the pool is $120$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n\n$D$\n\n.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nDetermine which of $x=5$, $x=10,$ and $x=15$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n$x-15$\n\nis a factor of $p(x)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe polynomial $p(x)$ can be factored as\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-15)\\cdot q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial. Find $q(x).$\n\n$q(x)=$\n$x^2+16x+240$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+16x+240=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q$, and solve the equation.\n\n$p+q\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,x=-8\\,\\pm \\,\\text{i}$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1113 #uuid "e1e3d509-88c2-5323-b14f-e4a8e86756a9" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248668 1145 "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+240 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+16x & =-240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x & =-240 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 8x+{8}^{2}-{8}^{2} & =-240 \\\\ (x+8{)}^{2}-64 & =-240 \\\\ (x+8{)}^{2} & =-176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8,q=-176,$ and $p+q=-168.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+8{)}^{2} & =-176 \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-176} \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-16\\cdot 11} \\\\ x+8 & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =-8\\pm \\text{i}\\,4\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-8+4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=-8-4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248669 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248670 13194139533726 true] [17592186248669 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248669 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248669 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248670 13194139533726 true] [17592186248669 1110 #uuid "bf50df35-234b-5876-af64-88ddd9354177" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248670 1034 "-168" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248670 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248670 1111 #uuid "50855d10-568e-56fa-9873-8d5832770518" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248671 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248672 13194139533726 true] [17592186248671 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248671 1041 "field-2" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248671 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248672 13194139533726 true] [17592186248671 1110 #uuid "2b941d34-4efe-549b-8beb-15b67fa1ff08" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248672 1034 "4\\sqrt{11}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248672 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248672 1111 #uuid "b457a84e-3d56-5863-87fe-772be86d4a6f" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248674 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1089 "q-167526" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/d6ac763e0d22c900ed29c43009d72c78.png)\n\nLucy has a small rectangular pool at home. The diagonal has a length of $x+2,$ one side of the rectangle has a length of $x,$ and the second side has a length of $a(x).$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nFind the expression for the length $a(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$a(x)=$\n$2\\sqrt{x+1}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/multistep-13931219/assets/q-167522.png)\n\nUsing the fact that the area of the pool is $120$ square feet, it can be shown that the value of $x$ is given by one of the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3\\,600.\n$$\n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the graph of $y=p(x)?$ Enter \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", or \"D\" (without quotes).\n\nThe diagram which shows $y=p(x)$ is\n\n$D$\n\n.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nDetermine which of $x=5$, $x=10,$ and $x=15$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n$x-15$\n\nis a factor of $p(x)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe polynomial $p(x)$ can be factored as\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-15)\\cdot q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $q(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial. Find $q(x).$\n\n$q(x)=$\n$x^2+16x+240$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+16x+240=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q$, and solve the equation.\n\n$p+q\\,=$\n$-168$\n$,\\,x=-8\\,\\pm \\,\\text{i}$\n$4\\sqrt{11}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe magnitude of the complex numbers found in part $5$ equals\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n." 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1113 #uuid "4e751724-4f02-5ee7-81f2-236db173dacd" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1138 false 13194139533726 true] [17592186248673 1145 "Let's pick the complex number $z=-8+4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i}.$\n\nFor a complex number $z=a+b\\text{i},$ the magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n|z|=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor the complex number $z=-8+4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i},$ we have $a=-8$ and $b=4\\sqrt{11}.$ So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}|-8+4\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{i}| & =\\sqrt{(-8{)}^{2}+(4\\sqrt{11}{)}^{2}} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}\\cdot 11} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}+{4}^{2}\\cdot 11} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}\\cdot (4+11)} \\\\ & =4\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248674 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248675 13194139533726 true] [17592186248674 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533726 true] [17592186248674 1041 "field-1" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248674 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248675 13194139533726 true] [17592186248674 1110 #uuid "20be51a4-3bba-5c73-bd1e-e989b8856935" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248675 1034 "4\\sqrt{15}" 13194139533726 true] [17592186248675 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533726 true] [17592186248675 1111 #uuid "1d98ef3c-cd8d-55a7-9eff-5f14d1ddb596" 13194139533726 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-167521" 17592186248656 "q-167521/field-1" 17592186248657 "q-167521/field-1/f7637684f266ac2e8a5d37e4c01402225bcd9ba7c8dd0cdfad38f678f9838b42" 17592186248658 "q-167522" 17592186248659 "q-167522/field-1" 17592186248660 "q-167522/field-1/885fe3edc6c725c68011b8798fb3574601671add7a0b04b375127f9789468b9f" 17592186248661